{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 8, :comments ["<b>And you shall sanctify him.</b> And it did not say ״and you shall sanctify״ following the pattern employed until now in the plural language. According to their words, of blessed memory (Yevamot 88b), this warning comes that if a priest did not wish to divorce a zona or a halalah etc., they flog him and discipline him until he divorces. And according to the reason the verse gives — <b>for the bread of your Elohim he offers</b> — one might reason that as long as there are whole priests and likewise a sufficient number of them to perform the offerings, there is no obligation to coerce; therefore it spoke in the singular — that even regarding one of them He is insistent upon carrying out this ruling." "<b>Holy he shall be to you.</b> Meaning, so that the priest may be holy, he shall be to you — meaning, behold he is yours, to coerce him in this matter. And the verse's saying <b>for holy</b> — meaning, because there has already preceded for us the knowledge that holiness is a quality acquired through will and through the fulfillment of desire and longing for it, and not through coercion — how can the verse say to coerce him regarding holiness? Therefore it says <b>for holy</b>: meaning, not to draw down holiness upon him anew, for he is already holy, but rather so as not to cause the loss of his holiness. And the verse's saying <b>I am HaShem who sanctifies you</b> gives the reason for Israel's obligation in this matter: for through the mediation of the priest's holiness, HaShem, blessed be He, causes His Shekhinah to dwell among us and sanctifies us; and if there is no priest, there is no avodah, and there is no mikdash, and there is no Indweller."], :book :leviticus, :voice :or-hachaim, :lang :en, :chapter 21, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T20:00:08.727934773Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}